.TH CPUMON 1 "2026-09-22" "cpumon 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpumon \- scrollable, man-page-styled Linux CPU usage monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpumon
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B cpumon \-\-once
[\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIcsv\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B cpumon \-\-daemon
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B cpumon \-\-replay
[\fB\-\-since\fR \fIWHEN\fR] [\fB\-\-until\fR \fIWHEN\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpumon
reads
.I /proc/stat
(aggregate and per-core user/nice/system/idle/iowait/irq/softirq/steal
jiffie counters, context switches, forks, running/blocked process counts)
along with
.IR /proc/loadavg ,
.IR /proc/uptime ,
.IR /proc/cpuinfo ,
CPU pressure stall information
.RI ( /proc/pressure/cpu ),
per-core frequency (cpufreq), CPU temperature sensors (hwmon and thermal
zones) and per-process CPU time
.RI ( /proc/[pid]/stat ),
and displays it either as a scrollable interactive terminal display that
auto-refreshes on a timer, or logs it in headless
.B \-\-daemon
mode, intended to run under systemd, to a daily file (text, JSON Lines or
CSV) and/or the systemd journal.
.PP
CPU usage is a rate, not a snapshot: every measurement is the delta between
two
.I /proc/stat
reads. The first frame of any mode is produced from a 200ms bootstrap
sample pair; subsequent frames use the delta between consecutive interval
ticks. Process CPU usage is reported like
.BR top (1):
percent of one CPU, so a process fully using four cores shows 400%.
.SH MODES
The default mode is the interactive ncurses display.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Headless mode: no interactive display, just logs a snapshot on each
interval. Intended for systemd. Handles SIGTERM/SIGINT for clean shutdown.
SIGHUP re-reads the config file, reopens the log file and restarts the
interval wait.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-once
Print a single snapshot to stdout (in \fB\-\-format\fR) and exit. No
interactive display, no loop, no log file written.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-plain
Interactive watch mode without ncurses: plain ANSI clear-screen/redraw
instead of full cursor addressing. Use this if the default ncurses
display looks garbled or fails to redraw in place on your terminal
(seen on some Windows/WSL terminal front-ends).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-replay
Print logged snapshots from the log directory to stdout and exit. Reads
every log format, compressed or not. Exits with status 1 if nothing
matched.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-since " " \fIWHEN\fR
.TQ
.BR \-u ", " \-\-until " " \fIWHEN\fR
Limit \fB\-\-replay\fR to snapshots at or after / at or before \fIWHEN\fR:
\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR, \fIYYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM\fR[\fI:SS\fR] (a \fBT\fR separator
also works), \fBtoday\fR, \fByesterday\fR, \fBnow\fR, or an interval
meaning that long ago (\fB30m\fR, \fB2h\fR, \fB7d\fR). A bare date given
to \fB\-\-until\fR means the end of that day.
.SH OPTIONS
Each setting's config-file/environment key is shown in brackets.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " " \fIINTERVAL\fR
[\fBCPUMON_INTERVAL\fR] Refresh/log interval. Accepts plain seconds or a
suffix: \fBs\fR (seconds), \fBm\fR (minutes), \fBh\fR (hours), \fBd\fR
(days). Examples: \fB5\fR, \fB5s\fR, \fB5m\fR, \fB1h\fR. Default: \fB5s\fR.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFMT\fR
[\fBCPUMON_FORMAT\fR] \fBtext\fR, \fBjson\fR or \fBcsv\fR. Applies to
\fB\-\-once\fR output and to log files, which are named
\fIYYYY\-MM\-DD.log\fR, \fI.jsonl\fR or \fI.csv\fR respectively. JSON logs
hold one object per line; CSV logs have a header row followed by one row
per snapshot. Default: \fBtext\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-top " " \fIN\fR
[\fBCPUMON_TOP\fR] Show and log the \fIN\fR processes that used the most CPU
during each interval (at most 50). \fB0\fR disables process scanning.
Default: \fB5\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alert " " \fIPCT\fR
[\fBCPUMON_ALERT_THRESHOLD\fR] Raise an alert when total CPU usage stays at
or above \fIPCT\fR percent for the alert duration, and clear it when usage
drops back below. Alerts go to syslog (and so the journal) and the log
file, and are shown as a banner in the interactive displays. \fB0\fR
disables alerts. Default: \fB0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-alert\-duration " " \fIDURATION\fR
[\fBCPUMON_ALERT_DURATION\fR] How long usage must stay high before alerting,
in interval syntax. Default: \fB0\fR (the first high sample).
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-log\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
[\fBCPUMON_LOG_DIR\fR] Directory for daily log files. Default:
\fI/var/log/cpumon\fR (falls back to \fI~/.local/share/cpumon\fR if that
is not writable).
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-log\-retention\-days " " \fIN\fR
[\fBCPUMON_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS\fR] Delete log files older than \fIN\fR days
automatically. \fBN=0\fR disables auto-deletion and keeps logs forever.
Default: \fB30\fR.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-compress\-after\-days " " \fIN\fR
[\fBCPUMON_COMPRESS_AFTER_DAYS\fR] gzip-compress log files that are at least
\fIN\fR days old (to \fI.gz\fR). \fBN=0\fR disables compression. Default:
\fB0\fR (the shipped \fI/etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf\fR sets \fB1\fR).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-log\-target " " \fITARGET\fR
[\fBCPUMON_LOG_TARGET\fR] \fBfile\fR (daily log files), \fBjournal\fR (the
systemd journal, with structured \fBCPUMON_*\fR fields and the full
snapshot as \fBCPUMON_JSON\fR) or \fBboth\fR. Default: \fBfile\fR.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-log
[\fBCPUMON_NO_LOG\fR] Disable logging while in interactive mode.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read settings from \fIFILE\fR instead of the default config file (see
\fBCONFIGURATION\fR). It is an error if \fIFILE\fR can't be read.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings are applied in layers: built-in defaults, then a config file,
then \fBCPUMON_*\fR environment variables, then command-line options.
.PP
The config file holds \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR lines using the keys listed
under \fBOPTIONS\fR; blank lines, \fB#\fR comments, an \fBexport\fR prefix
and quoted values are accepted, and invalid lines are reported and skipped.
\fB\-\-daemon\fR reads \fI/etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf\fR; the interactive modes
and \fB\-\-once\fR read \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cpumon/cpumon.conf\fR
(default \fI~/.config/cpumon/cpumon.conf\fR); \fB\-\-replay\fR reads both.
Missing default files are ignored.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B "up/down or j/k"
Scroll one line.
.TP
.B "PgUp/PgDn"
Scroll one page.
.TP
.B "g / G"
Jump to top / bottom of the report.
.TP
.B r
Refresh immediately and reset the countdown.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop cleanly.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
In \fB\-\-daemon\fR mode, re-read the config file and reopen the log file
(\fBsystemctl reload cpumon\fR sends this). In the interactive modes, quit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/cpumon/YYYY\-MM\-DD.{log,jsonl,csv}[.gz]
Default daily log location (see \fB\-\-log\-dir\fR). Files older than the
retention window are deleted and files older than
\fB\-\-compress\-after\-days\fR are gzip-compressed, whenever the log rolls
over to a new day and when the daemon starts or reloads. Only files matching
this pattern are ever touched.
.TP
.I /etc/cpumon/cpumon.conf
Daemon configuration. Edit and run \fBsystemctl reload cpumon\fR to apply.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cpumon/cpumon.conf
Per-user configuration for the interactive modes and \fB\-\-once\fR.
.TP
.I /lib/systemd/system/cpumon.service
systemd unit for running cpumon as a headless logging daemon.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
cpumon
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 seconds.
.TP
cpumon \-i 5m \-t 10
Interactive display, refreshing every 5 minutes, showing the 10 busiest
processes.
.TP
cpumon \-\-once \-\-format json
One-shot report as a JSON object.
.TP
cpumon \-\-daemon \-i 5m \-a 90 \-A 10m
Headless logger that alerts when usage stays at or above 90% for 10 minutes.
.TP
cpumon \-\-replay \-\-since yesterday \-\-until today
Print yesterday's and today's logged snapshots.
.TP
journalctl \-u cpumon \-o json | jq \-r .CPUMON_TOTAL
Total CPU usage from snapshots logged with \fB\-\-log\-target journal\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR mpstat (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR proc (5)
